    <figcaption>Cummins, Thorberg and Theis Company, Main Street, 1908</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Around 1880, Thorberg became a clerk at Hanover and Barth Clothiers. He remained until 1887, when he joined Granberry and Cummins Company. After Granberry's death, Cummins brought in new partners and renamed the store Cummins and Tubbs. Thorberg acquired a working interest, bought out Tubbs in 1891, and became a named partner. With the later addition of {% person "j-h-theis" %}J. H. Theis{% endperson %}, the business operated for decades as Cummins, Thorberg and Theis.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/andrew-e-thorberg/177-andrew-e-thorberg-300-dpi.jpg" alt="Portrait of Andrew E. Thorberg">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>{% person "benjamin-harrison" %}President Benjamin Harrison{% endperson %} appointed Thorberg postmaster of Mandan in 1890. He resigned in 1894 because of other pressing responsibilities. Thorberg retired from active business in 1914, disposed of his retail stock in 1916, and invested in farmland purchased from {% person "peter-hendrickson" %}Peter Hendrickson{% endperson %}, {% person "leonard-johnson" %}Leonard Johnson{% endperson %}, and {% person "alfred-johnson" %}Alfred Johnson{% endperson %}.</p>

<p>In 1917, he opened the Thorberg Tile Company on the Old Fort Lincoln flats. The company made structural tile, rough-clay products, and high-grade brick. Thorberg had experimented with fruit and trees in his Mandan garden and became a determined advocate for a federal dryland agricultural experiment station in the region.</p>

<p>Thorberg helped found the Morton County Fair Association and establish Mandan's original fairgrounds. He supervised an Indigenous exhibit at the 1893 Chicago World's Fair and represented Mandan at congressional hearings seeking an Indian school for the city.</p>

<p>Frequent contact through his business helped Thorberg learn the Sioux language, and he became acquainted with {% person "sitting-bull" %}Sitting Bull{% endperson %}. He was among those who accompanied Sitting Bull to the 1893 World's Fair. Thorberg also helped organize Mandan's first commercial club, the Mandan Business Men's Association. Although active in politics, he held no other political office after serving as postmaster.</p>

<p>Thorberg married {% person "ida-karoline-johnson-thorberg" %}Ida Karoline Johnson{% endperson %}, who was born March 1, 1864, in Drogsta, Södermanland, Sweden. Their eight children—{% person "eric-a-thorberg" %}Eric A.{% endperson %}, {% person "clara-thorberg-higgins" %}Clara Higgins{% endperson %}, {% person "george-e-thorberg" %}George E.{% endperson %}, {% person "harry-s-thorberg" %}Harry S.{% endperson %}, {% person "lilly-thorberg-kleinschmitt" %}Lilly Kleinschmitt{% endperson %}, {% person "ida-thorberg-diebert" %}Ida Diebert{% endperson %}, {% person "clifford-thorberg" %}Clifford{% endperson %}, and {% person "robert-v-thorberg" %}Robert V.{% endperson %}—were born in Mandan.</p>

<p>Andrew Thorberg died February 24, 1921, and is buried beside Ida in Mandan's Union Cemetery. This biography is based largely on his entry in <em>Morton County Roots</em>, published by the Morton County Historical Society in 1976.</p>
